% Lavignac, Encyclopédie de la musique, Part I vol. 5 (1922): Raouf Yekta Bey on Turkish music, Jules Rouanet on Arab music in the Maghreb.
% Published early from the workshop on 11 November 2026: each tune names what is guessed or left out in its N: lines,
% and its workshop record (or review item) is kept for further correction.

X:1
T:Adana melody
T:Peasant melody from Adana: hearing a neutral second
C:traditional (Turkey), author unknown
O:Turkey, Adana province (Çukurova)
R:folk melody
S:Raouf Yekta Bey, "La musique turque", Lavignac, Encyclopédie de la musique, Part I vol. 5 (1922), p. 2951
N:A folk melody Raouf Yekta collected among the peasants of Adana province and printed to answer Europeans who heard such tunes as A minor: it is built on the tetrachord A B C D with a B lower than the European one, and he gives its exact place (A to B 65536/59049, B to C 2187/2048, C to D 9/8, so B sits about 20 cents below the piano's B). The tuning line plays his B, C and D and a pure E; the other notes stay in equal temperament. Play it tuned, then untuned, and ask the class which B is "the right one".
N:No words were printed: play it on the recorder or hum it. The last two notes of the next-to-last bar (G natural, F sharp) are a close reading of small print. The drum is ours: the Sofyan usul (düm, tek-ka), a two-beat Turkish cycle, not Yekta's choice for this tune. Whether the mode is Uşşak or Hüseyni waits for a Turkish musician.
M:2/4
L:1/32
Q:1/4=56
K:Am
