X:2
T:B'qāw ʿala khēr
T:Stay well: the Maghreb's closing song
C:traditional (Algeria and the Maghreb)
O:Algiers, Algeria
R:festival song (leave-taking)
S:Jules Rouanet, "La musique arabe dans le Maghreb", Lavignac, Encyclopédie de la musique, Part I vol. 5 (1922), p. 2824
N:The closing song of every Maghreb feast with music: the musicians' goodbye to the hosts who listened. Every couplet begins "Stay well!", and when the old couplets run out the singers make up lines naming the guests. In class: the goodbye song at the end of a lesson, the first line sung by all, then a couplet for each child's name; Rāna jīnāk is its hello song.
N:Words as Rouanet spelled them (a French-style transliteration of Algerian Arabic): "Stay well! I want to go home: the muezzin's voice is in the sky (it is dawn) ... Ah! Stay well!" The muezzin only marks the hour the feast ends; the song is not a religious text. The fit of "fes-sa-ma-i ci Ah!" to the notes is approximate, as he prints it; the E flats in bars 8 and 10-11 follow the key signature. Rouanet writes 6/8 (pairs of these 3/8 bars) and returns to the sign for each new couplet; the drum is his: high, low, high, low by eighths. An Arabic speaker should still check the words.
M:3/8
L:1/8
Q:3/8=108
K:Bb
