X:3
T:Rāna jīnāk
T:We have come to you: the Maghreb's opening song
C:traditional (Algeria and the Maghreb)
O:Algiers, Algeria
R:festival song (processional)
S:Jules Rouanet, "La musique arabe dans le Maghreb", Lavignac, Encyclopédie de la musique, Part I vol. 5 (1922), p. 2824
N:"Perhaps the best-known tune of the Maghreb" (Rouanet): the song that opens a feast (weddings, births, processions), played outdoors by shawms (ghaita) and drums at the head of the procession and at home by the women's ensemble of violin, frame drum and goblet drum. In class: a hello song as everyone arrives, with B'qāw ʿala khēr as its goodbye.
N:Words as Rouanet spelled them (French-style transliteration of Algerian Arabic), "Rana djinak, ya dou aïni ... Nari! Ya nari!": "We have come to you, light of my eyes ... My fire! O my fire!", a cry of joy. The rest of each couplet was improvised good wishes, and only these words are printed. Guessed: the last sixteenth of bar 8 (B) and where "Ya" falls in bar 15. The tempo is ours for his "Allegro"; the drum is his rhythm, high strokes on the rim and low on the bass drum. An Arabic speaker should still check the words.
M:2/4
L:1/16
Q:1/4=112
